Doubled-rate rungs (8341 B/s): 51/2 → 73.99 %, 103/4 → 88.31 %, 207/8 → 88.78 %.

**An 832 B frame at 5 Hz loses 34 % while a 52 B frame at 80 Hz loses 0.81 % — identical
bytes per second.** Slowing down does NOT rescue a large frame. Mechanism: frames bigger
than one BLE connection event get fragmented, and one lost fragment kills the whole frame,
so whole-frame loss climbs with size. **Neither byte-rate nor packet-rate limited — packet
SIZE limited.**

**Consequence for the "log more variables without real time" plan (user's goal): the
big-slow-frame trade is DEAD, but the goal is still reachable by ROUND-ROBIN.** Keep frames
at ~52 B and cycle the variable set across successive frames: 80.2 Hz x ~10 usable floats
≈ **800 floats/s**, i.e. **100 variables at 8 Hz** or **200 at 4 Hz**. That is what the
multi-slot `0x21` subscribe path already does, and it wants small frames anyway.
**Do not build a wide-frame logger.**

